Z||000011||Sootah||09-30-2001||07:08 PM||||I'm looking at having the same problem. Going to SLC tommorow for oral surgery and going to look at a Fiero w/ the headlights stuck up. How hard are the motors to replace (If this is required)? Or either of the relays. The guy says that he either has to have the headlights up or down, they wont do either on their own.<P>Also, how hard are the locks to replace? He also says that the drivers side door lock no longer works, can I do this myself, or if not how much will it cost? (Hey, the car is only $1000 and is mint otherwise supposedly.) Ah, yes, its an '85 GT.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Nuclear_Happy_Face_Sig.jpg">  <BR><sup><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter</sup>||||reg||1
Z||000012||mbramble||09-30-2001||07:40 PM||||Headlight motors and relays are easy to replace if you need to do so.  Rodney Dickman also has replacement parts for the old style (like your 85) motors.<P>To replace the door lock (Which you can buy from the Fiero Store, other places as wel probably), remove the inner door panel, reach in and pull the big retaining clip off the lock, unhook the lock rod.  Insert the new lock, put in the retaining clip and reconnect the lock rod.  Very easy job -- except for the fatc that you have little room to work thru the door.  But, it's just time consuming, not difficult....<BR>||||reg||1
